\subsection{Reproducibility and Controls}
\label{sec:reproducibility}

We assess measurement reliability through an intraclass correlation analysis: 30 policy clauses $\times$ 3 independent replicates $\times$ 2 models yield ICC $= 0.919$ (95\% CI $[0.892, 0.940]$), which falls in the ``excellent'' range. Per-type ICC values mirror the layer structure: linguistic-layer types achieve higher reliability (scopal 0.989, lexical 0.981, coreferential 0.976) than specification-layer types (authorization scope 0.857, conditional precedence 0.856, incompleteness 0.807). This pattern is compatible with the greater interpretive latitude that specification-layer ambiguities afford: models are more likely to vary across replicates when the policy leaves substantive conditions unspecified.

The perturbation control (\S\ref{sec:results}, $n = 120$) provides a complementary validity check. Synonym substitution and clause reordering without genuine ambiguity produce $\Delta_{\text{perturb}} = 3.33$ pp (CI $[-5.0, +13.33]$, contains zero), an order of magnitude below the 34.0 pp ambiguity effect. Together, the high ICC and the null perturbation result establish that our measurement captures a stable, ambiguity-specific phenomenon.

